{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce a negotiated agreement and explicitly claim it 'resulted in the following provisions being included in the Collins Lieberman legislation' — a claim that their provisions were adopted/included in legislation, which clarification (2) treats as credit-claiming ('had amendments/provisions adopted or included in moving legislation'). The release also touts Lott's personal influence ('His insight and input have been extremely valuable... The Senators suggestions have improved and strengthened our bill'), fitting clarification (4) on touting a concrete role. I note some ambiguity because the bill was only being introduced and no passage or delivered benefit is claimed, which is close to the negative rule on merely introduced legislation; however, the explicit claim that their compromise provisions were incorporated goes beyond simple authorship of an introduced bill."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed as an informational column about the U.S. Marshals Service, the senator explicitly states: 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' This is an explicit 'I helped secure' claim of delivered federal funding, squarely within clarification (4) (touting past accomplishments) and the rule that a release qualifies if it contains an explicit credit claim even if its main purpose is something else."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Much of the speech recap is position-taking (Iraq, intelligence, taxes), but Bond explicitly claims the 'new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' This claims co-authorship of enacted legislation delivering concrete funds to his state, fitting clarification (2) and (4) (touting past accomplishments). Per the clarification, a release qualifies if it contains an explicit credit claim even if its main purpose is something else."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby, identified as a member of the Appropriations Committee, 'announced that the Department of Homeland Security will release $3,510 to the Homewood Fire Department.' Per clarification (1), announcements of federal grants benefiting the senator's state count as credit-claiming because the announcement associates the senator with the benefit, especially with words like 'announced.' No claim of personal causation is needed under this rule."
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "DeWine announces a $105,550 grant to the Parma Fire Department and explicitly claims personal responsibility for the program delivering it: he 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' 'helped secure approximately $650 million for the FIRE Act,' and 'passed an updated version of the bill.' This combines a grant announcement with authorship/funding-securing claims — the archetype of credit-claiming under clarification (1), (2), and (4)."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a floor statement criticizing Democrats over judicial nomination filibusters and defending the 'constitutional option.' Dole claims no government action, benefit, or legislative accomplishment; she only takes a position and commends Leader Frist (crediting another official). This is pure position-taking under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison signs on as an original cosponsor of a bill that was just 'introduced today.' No enactment, passage, adoption, or delivered benefit is claimed. Under negative clarification (2), cosponsoring pending legislation is a stance, not an accomplishment."
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez issues a statement on pending House legislation: he applauds Congressman Putnam (crediting another official), states he cannot support the bill in its current form, and threatens a filibuster. This is position-taking on pending legislation with no claim of having produced any government action or benefit — negative clarifications (1), (2), and (6)."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe announces that $8.1 million for the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard was included in the appropriations bill that 'passed the Senate today,' and states 'This funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins.' She also notes she 'sent a letter to Appropriators earlier this year requesting additional funding' for veterans programs included in the bill. This juxtaposes her requests with delivered funding in a passed bill — credit-claiming under clarification (1) and (3), similar to the Talent positive example."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The main purpose is an essay contest (ceremonial), but the release touts that 'One of Senator Bayh's first pieces of legislation in the Senate was the Responsible Fatherhood Act, parts of which were signed into the law by President Bush earlier this year,' and that as governor he 'more than doubled the amount of money collected for child support payments.' The clarification expressly says a release qualifies if it contains an explicit credit claim 'even if its main purpose is something else (e.g., an essay contest... that also touts enacted legislation)' — this is exactly that scenario."
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords's statement is purely a vote explanation: 'I voted in support of the Sens. Kerry and Levin amendments today because...' It expresses a position on Iraq policy and criticizes the Administration, claiming no role in producing any government action or benefit. This is pure position-taking under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Identical to the codebook's positive example: the release announces that the Senate adopted 'the Nelson amendment' 72-26, and Nelson calls it 'a victory for Nebraska's ranchers.' He claims personal responsibility for a legislative outcome benefiting constituents — clarification (2) (amendment adopted in moving legislation)."
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is the same text as the codebook's negative example: Kennedy introduces 'Medicare for All' legislation and argues the policy case at length. Because the bill is only being introduced and no enactment, passage, or delivered benefit is claimed, it is position-taking on pending legislation under negative clarification (2)."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed as a hearing announcement, the release claims concrete results from the Subcommittee's oversight: 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed by 4,415% from a mere $680,000 in 2003 to $31 million in 2006 alone,' and 'levies on payments to deadbeat federal contractors have jumped 746%, raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' Clarification (5) counts oversight/investigative efforts claiming concrete results (money recovered) as credit-claiming."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kyl co-sponsors an amendment that 'would add' 2,000 Border Patrol agents and 'calls for' $352 million; the release never claims the amendment was adopted, passed, or delivered any benefit — it remains a proposal ('The time to beef up our defenses is now'). Under negative clarification (2), sponsoring pending legislation with no claimed enactment, passage, or adoption is a stance, not an accomplishment."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The main action — a letter urging Secretary Chertoff to consider a levee proposal — is advocacy with no claimed result (negative clarification (5)). However, the release also states 'Sen. Cornyn added an amendment to the Department of State and Foreign Operations Appropriations bill that required the IBWC... to report to Congress,' claiming his amendment was included/adopted in moving appropriations legislation with an operative requirement, and that he 'has made several efforts... to see that federal funding is increased.' Under clarification (2) (provisions/amendments adopted or included in moving legislation) and the rule that any explicit credit claim qualifies the release, I coded this 1. I note some ambiguity because the letter itself is result-free and the amendment's ultimate fate is unstated, but 'added an amendment... that required' reads as a claim of a successful legislative action."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the headline action is introducing a new bill (which alone would not qualify), the release touts a past accomplishment: 'she helped win a two-year change in the Economic Growth Reconciliation Act... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million above the traditional Medicaid formula amount.' This 'helped win' claim of delivered federal funds fits clarification (4) (touting past accomplishments), and a release qualifies if it contains an explicit credit claim even if its main purpose is the new bill."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott announces that FEMA is moving 300 trailers and 100 mobile homes to Mississippi, and the release explicitly juxtaposes this with his prior intervention: 'The announcement follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush to authorize the immediate transport of available trailers.' This is clarification (3) — juxtaposing the senator's appeal with a subsequent favorable agency action, implying causation — and thus credit-claiming rather than merely welcoming another's decision."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison asked questions at a hearing, 'received assurances,' and 'expressed her support' for the Army's budget request; she says she has 'supported increasing the Army's end strength and am encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental.' She never claims to have secured the funding or caused any decision — supporting and being 'encouraged to see' funds is welcoming action by others. This fits negative clarifications (4) (welcoming decisions made by others with no claim of involvement) and (5) (oversight hearings with no claimed result)."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the release's purpose is to urge conferees to retain the provision, it explicitly claims a legislative accomplishment: 'Last month, the Senate unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Richard Durbin' — repeatedly called 'his legislation.' Under clarification (2), claiming authorship of an amendment that has passed a chamber is credit-claiming, and a release qualifies if it contains an explicit credit claim even if its main purpose is advocacy for future action."
} }

{
  "133": {
    "document_id": "133",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry's statement criticizes the Dubai Ports deal and the Administration's handling of it, citing the parent company's participation in the Israel boycott. He claims no role and no delivered benefit. Under negative clarification (1), this is pure position-taking and criticism of the administration."
  },
  "214": {
    "document_id": "214",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a Pearl Harbor remembrance honoring those who served and died. It is commemorative content with no claim of any governmental action or benefit. Under negative clarification (3), commemorations are not credit-claiming."
  },
  "299": {
    "document_id": "299",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Alexander announces he received a 'Hero of the Taxpayer' award from Americans for Tax Reform based on his votes. Under negative clarification (3), awards the senator receives are ceremonial/honorary content, not credit-claiming. His statement of pride in siding with taxpayers is position-taking about votes, not a claim of producing a government benefit."
  },
  "490": {
    "document_id": "490",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Webb's statement responds to the NIE on Iran by criticizing the Bush administration's refusal to engage adversaries. It claims no personal accomplishment or delivered benefit; it is pure position-taking and criticism under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "630": {
    "document_id": "630",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd's statement comments on the Connecticut impact of Bush's proposed budget, praising Connecticut manufacturers and urging the administration to fund two additional submarines per year. This is advocacy for future action and commentary on a proposal, not a claim of having secured any benefit. Under negative clarification (1), it is position-taking."
  },
  "721": {
    "document_id": "721",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement on Iraq salutes the elections but criticizes the president for lacking a plan and promises Democrats will keep asking hard questions. It is pure position-taking and criticism with no claim of responsibility for any government action or benefit, falling under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "773": {
    "document_id": "773",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage of a resolution honoring the life of the late Senator Eagleton, which McCaskill and colleagues introduced. Although the resolution passed, it is purely honorary/commemorative content. Under negative clarification (3), commemorations are not credit-claiming; the recitation of Eagleton's accomplishments concerns another official, not the issuing senator."
  },
  "1060": {
    "document_id": "1060",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that the Senate passed SCHIP reauthorization and that 'Nebraska's Senator's Small Business Health Care Amendment' was included in the legislation: 'The legislation also includes an amendment co sponsored by Senator Nelson.' Under clarification (2), claims of sponsorship of amendments or provisions adopted or included in legislation that passed a chamber count as credit-claiming. The headline's 'Amendment Included' framing and 'Nelson has long pushed for such legislation' claim personal responsibility for a concrete legislative outcome."
  },
  "1086": {
    "document_id": "1086",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dayton applauds the Senate's passage of additional LIHEAP funding but claims no personal responsibility for it; indeed, the release emphasizes that four amendments he cosponsored to fully fund the program 'were defeated.' Under negative clarification (4), welcoming a decision made by others with no claim of advocacy or involvement in the delivered benefit is not credit-claiming, and his noted involvement concerns failed amendments, not the passed funding."
  },
  "1155": {
    "document_id": "1155",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement is mostly a call for future work on an asbestos trust fund, but it contains an explicit backward-looking credit claim: 'With the unanimous adoption of the Leahy Hatch medical criteria amendment this committee reached consensus on the proper standards for determining legitimate victims.' Under clarification (2), claims of authorship/sponsorship of amendments that were adopted or advanced in committee count as credit-claiming, and the codebook states a release qualifies if it contains an explicit credit claim even if its main purpose is something else. I weighed negative clarification (5) (hearings with no claimed result), but a result—the unanimous adoption of his named amendment—is claimed."
  },
  "1313": {
    "document_id": "1313",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lieberman expresses frustration that the House will not take up the D.C. voting rights bill. The bill was authored by Davis and Norton, not Lieberman; he commends others' efforts and hopes for future action. No enactment, passage, or delivered benefit is claimed, and no personal accomplishment is asserted. Under negative clarifications (1) and (6), this is position-taking crediting others."
  },
  "1827": {
    "document_id": "1827",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that provisions of 'Sen. Dick Lugar's Good Samaritan Hunger Relief Act will be included in the Grassley-Baucus tax relief package' for Katrina victims, with Lugar appreciating the chairmen for 'including this important provision.' Under clarification (2), claims of authorship of provisions adopted or included in moving legislation count as credit-claiming. The release associates Lugar's authored provisions with a concrete legislative vehicle delivering tax benefits for food donations."
  },
  "2003": {
    "document_id": "2003",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline and framing—'Senator Clinton Announces New Predator Squadron for New York'—constitute an announcement of a base/mission decision benefiting the senator's state, which clarification (1) expressly includes as credit-claiming 'because the announcement associates the senator with the benefit, especially with words like... announced.' I considered negative clarification (4) (welcoming decisions made by others), since Clinton says she is 'pleased' with the Air Force decision without explicitly claiming she secured it; however, this release is itself the delivery of the good news to New York (citing jobs and economic growth for the state), not a reaction to previously announced news, so the inclusion rule governs."
  },
  "2143": {
    "document_id": "2143",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Murray's statement mourns the passing of former state party chair Karen Marchioro and offers condolences to her family. Under negative clarification (3), condolences are ceremonial content, not credit-claiming; no government action or benefit is claimed."
  },
  "3047": {
    "document_id": "3047",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release (from Sen. Craig's office) announces USDA's purchase of up to 80 million pounds of potatoes and immediately notes that 'On March 15, 2005, the Idaho Congressional Delegation wrote the Secretary requesting this action,' linking to the letter. Under clarification (3), juxtaposing the delegation's (including the issuing senator's) request with the subsequent favorable agency action implies causation and constitutes credit-claiming for a concrete benefit to Idaho potato growers."
  },
  "3201": {
    "document_id": "3201",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Talent announces USDA's disaster designation for Missouri and is described as having 'worked with USDA officials to expedite the approval,' and the release also recalls his earlier announcement of $1,078,500 in ECP funds for Missouri. Under clarification (1), announcements of disaster designations benefiting the senator's state count as credit-claiming, and clarification (3) covers juxtaposing his intervention with the favorable agency action."
  },
  "3914": {
    "document_id": "3914",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Frist's statement on job numbers credits 'Republican economic policies' for growth and attacks Democrats on taxes. Under negative clarification (6), crediting a party rather than claiming personal responsibility is not credit-claiming; it is also pure position-taking under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "3950": {
    "document_id": "3950",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The visible text quotes Harkin hoping a new regional diabetes facility will help Iowans and describes him as a leader on health who 'recently authored the HeLP America Act.' There is no claim that he secured the facility or any funding; the HeLP America Act is only introduced legislation, which under negative clarification (2) is a stance, not an accomplishment. Welcoming the facility without any claim of involvement also fits negative clarification (4)."
  },
  "4124": {
    "document_id": "4124",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy commends President Bush for calling for comprehensive immigration reform and urges turning 'talk into action.' This is praise of another official plus a call for future action, with no enacted benefit or claimed accomplishment. Under negative clarifications (1) and (4), it is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4344": {
    "document_id": "4344",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar announces he joined a bipartisan group supporting the Senate-White House PATRIOT Act compromise and expresses approval of its provisions (e.g., library protections), but he claims no authorship or responsibility for those provisions—'I am pleased the compromise includes provisions' rather than 'I secured.' Under negative clarifications (1) and (2), supporting pending compromise legislation without claiming adoption of one's provisions is position-taking."
  }
}
